>
> I take it armor was considered but there was a decision it would be too
> powerful.  Or they didn't want to code it for that many materials.

It wouldn't have to be coded individually for each kind of armor.  You
just make it work for anything in the "armor" item class that's rusted
or corroded (but, presumably, not rotted or burnt, because that
wouldn't even make sense).  My guess would be that it was deemed
unnecessarily powerful.  Damaged armor is supposed to be at least
somewhat of a pain to fix, because most of the things that damage it
are otherwise rather wimpy.

Note that damaged armor *can* be repaired, you just have to spend a
scroll of enchant armor.  These tend to be in short supply, because
usually you typically want to enchant at least five pieces of armor,
maybe more, up to +5 before the endgame.  Needing to use up a scroll
kind of hurts, so you try to damage-proof your armor ASAP -- which you
can do with the otherwise useless scroll of _destroy_ armor.
Balancing these needs and the availability of the scrolls (and in some
cases holy water) is a significant aspect of the middle game.

It wouldn't be quite the same dynamic with weapons, for several
reasons.

First, you don't need as many scrolls of enchant weapon, because you
don't need to enchant five different weapons.  I can imagine feeling
the need to significantly enchant possibly as many as three distinct
weapons, counting a big fat stack of projectiles as one, if you want
to #twoweapon and also use projectiles.  (This leaves out the
possibility of enchanting something in the early-middle game that you
end up not using later because you eventually get something better,
but that happens with armor as well -- possibly with armor for
multiple different slots.)

Second, you can't rustproof weapons with scrolls of destroy weapon,
because there's no such scroll.  So you're going to use a scroll of
enchant weapon to do that.

Third, it's relatively quick and easy to switch to an alternate weapon
for dealing with things that cause damage.  Switching out all your
armor every time would be a lot more tedious, not to mention dangerous
and, with anything that's at all quick, frequently impossible.

Taken all together, allowing oil to repair the damage to weapons
doesn't really change the game that much.  The main thing it does is
create an extra use for oil, which is good, because oil is not one of
the most highly useful potions (although, neither is it so under-
useful and over-available as "Your 34 potions of object detection
dilute further.")  Allowing the same thing for armor would have a much
greater impact on balance and difficulty.  Other aspects of the game
would have to be adjusted, possibly rather significantly, to
compensate.


